WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.030
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.190
I'm Maurice Samuels, the director

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.970
of the Yale Program for
the Study of Antisemitism.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.620
And I'm really happy to
welcome you here today

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.190
to our Benjamin and Barbara
Zucker Lecture Series.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.690
Today's speaker, Mark Oppenheimer,

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.770
is the author of five books,

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.177
including "Knocking on Heaven's Door:

00:00:26.177 --> 00:00:29.560
"American Religion in the
Age of Counterculture,"

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.190
which was based on his 2003 dissertation

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.620
in Yale's Religious Studies Department,

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.010
winner of that year's Porter Prize.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.530
He was the religion columnist

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.630
for "The New York Times""

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.410
from 2010 to 2016,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.607
and has written for "The
New York Times Magazine,"

00:00:45.607 --> 00:00:47.980
"GQ," "Mother Jones," "The Nation"

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.000
and "The Atlantic,"
among other publications.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.900
The host of "Tablet Magazine's"
podcast "Unorthodox,"

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.070
Oppenheimer has taught
at Stanford, Wellesley

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.670
and Yale, where since 2006,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.450
he has directed the Yale
Journalism Initiative.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.690
He lives with his family

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:04.830
in New Haven, Connecticut,

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.940
and currently teaches Daily Themes

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.540
in the English Department at Yale.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.057
Last October, he published his new book,

00:01:12.057 --> 00:01:15.017
"Squirrel Hill: The Tree
of Life Synagogue Shooting

00:01:15.017 --> 00:01:17.070
"and the Soul of a Neighborhood,"

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.800
which he researched with funding

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.010
from our program.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.830
And that is also the
title of his talk today.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.240
So let me just say a word about
how things are gonna work.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.140
Mark will speak for about 30 minutes,

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.750
and then we'll have about 20

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.780
to 25 minutes of questions

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.800
from the audience.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.670
So please submit your questions

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.440
using the Q and A function

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.210
at the bottom of your screen.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.430
Don't raise your hand
because we can't see you.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:45.950
Now, (laughs) please join me

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.103
in welcoming Mark Oppenheimer.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.250
- Maurice, thank you so much.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.810
It's a great honor to be here.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.750
And I would, first of all,

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.980
just very quickly like to thank

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.780
the Yale Program for the
Study of Antisemitism

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.260
for funding this project, in part,

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:04.930
and now for having me back

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.780
to talk to all of you about it,

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.780
and Maurice and Inessa Laskova

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.958
at the department, at the program.

00:02:11.958 --> 00:02:14.160
I also wanna thank the Zucker
family for having endowed

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.390
this lecture series that's
having me here today.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.840
As it happens, I have a history

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.990
with Ben Zucker because he,

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.760
among other things, in
addition to being himself

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.760
the author of some very fine books,

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.510
he is a jeweler who, years ago,

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.670
provided the setting
for the engagement ring

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.810
that I gave my wife.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.310
And so far, the marriage has lasted,

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.223
so I figure, it all worked out well,

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:38.240
all's well that ends well.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.080
So I haven't seen him in a while,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.170
but it's good to be here,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.860
and I hope he's here today.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.100
I actually know that he tried to reach out

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.950
to me yesterday, and I
wasn't able to connect,

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.560
but it's good to be back with him

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.983
in that sense.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.970
So I wanna say a little bit today

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.290
about the book,

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.410
and then I'm going to read

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.600
two pages from it.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.520
Then I'm gonna share about
a dozen photographs from it

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.590
because it is a fairly
richly illustrated book,

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.288
which was very important to me.

00:03:06.288 --> 00:03:08.010
And then I'm gonna read
one or two more pages.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.440
And all told, that's gonna take
about 1/2 an hour, at most.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.270
And then I really wanna leave ample time

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.010
for all of your questions,
because I think that's,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.880
for me, certainly, (audio cuts out) part.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.710
I've heard myself talk a lot
over the past few months,

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.180
and I haven't heard
from most of you at all.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.100
And I think every time I speak,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.780
I get new questions and new provocations

00:03:27.915 --> 00:03:30.615
and people who enrich my own
thinking about this topic

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:32.810
along the way.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.880
So let me first tell you where I was

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.760
on October 27th, 2018, in the morning,

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.150
which was the morning

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.870
of the deadliest antisemitic attack

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.560
in American history,
when 11 Jews were killed

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.820
inside the Tree of Life
Synagogue building,

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.220
out of the 22 people

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.440
who were inside the building that day.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.311
So there were 22 people,

00:03:50.311 --> 00:03:52.763
1/2 of whom did not make it out alive.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:55.633
It was

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.110
a cold October morning

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.450
in Newton, Massachusetts,

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.830
where I was with my daughter,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:05.820
my eldest daughter.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.800
She had been invited to the bat mitzvah

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.663
of a summer camp friend of hers.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.330
And so we were up at Temple Emanuel

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.660
in Newton, Massachusetts, two hours or so

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.140
from our home in New Haven.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.840
We were inside synagogue all morning,

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.650
and so we did not

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:22.483
have technology with us.

00:04:22.483 --> 00:04:25.110
We don't bring phones into
synagogue on the Sabbath,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:26.230
in our family.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:28.300
And I assume that because
there were hundreds

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.133
of people inside the building,

00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:31.920
and it's not an extremely
religious congregation,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.870
there were certainly people
there who had their phones out

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.560
at some point and probably knew

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:36.710
what was happening in Pittsburgh,

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.240
but if so, they didn't tell us,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.210
and Rebecca and I didn't know.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.150
When we got back out to my car,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.910
in the parking lot, at
about one in the afternoon,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.600
I did open up my phone

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.010
to see if my wife had reached us,

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:51.460
and I had all these text
messages from people,

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.310
saying, "Are you going to Pittsburgh?

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.310
"What's do you know about Pittsburgh?

00:04:57.157 --> 00:04:58.820
"Can you find out more about Pittsburgh?"

00:04:58.820 --> 00:04:59.910
And I thought, why are all these people

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.400
texting me about Pittsburgh?

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.780
So then I navigated over to my news app

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.260
and I saw what had happened.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.310
And I must have looked
very, very stricken,

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:10.313
because my daughter Rebecca said to me,

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.880
she said, "Dad, is everything okay?"

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.080
And I said, "Well, no."

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.683
I said, "There's been a shooting,

00:05:21.599 --> 00:05:24.087
"and some Jews are dead in Squirrel Hill."

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.617
And Rebecca said, "Squirrel Hill?

00:05:28.617 --> 00:05:30.840
"Isn't that where we're from?"

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.100
And I said, "Well, in a sense, yes."

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.140
Because what she understood,

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.850
even though she herself is from New Haven

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.610
and I am from Springfield, Massachusetts,

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.390
she knew that her grandpa, my dad,

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:43.390
was from Squirrel Hill.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:44.760
And I think she may even have known

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.740
from family lore that he
was the third generation

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.030
to be from the Squirrel Hill neighborhood

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.280
of Pittsburgh, and the fifth generation

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:52.160
to be from Pittsburgh, in fact.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:53.960
So the Oppenheimers got to Pittsburgh

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.930
in the 1840s, and were in other parts,

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.830
slightly western parts of the city,

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:00.670
for two generations.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.170
And then as soon as
Squirrel Hill was developed,

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:03.590
around the time of World War I,

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.540
they settled in Squirrel Hill
and had been there ever since.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:08.860
So it was the family and
is the family homeland.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:10.473
It's where her people are from.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:13.760
So we were very, very, very torn apart.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.260
And over the coming days,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.700
I thought a lot about what it meant

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:18.810
to be from this neighborhood.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.750
And increasingly, my mind kept going

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:23.060
to the neighborhood itself,

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.330
because although there
have been very good books

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.450
about the perpetrators

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:31.350
of antisemitic attacks

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.133
and of mass terror, indeed,

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.200
and mass shootings of which
there have been hundreds

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.550
in the United States

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:40.140
since Columbine in 1999,

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.180
what I realized I didn't
know enough about,

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:45.030
and what I immediately got curious about

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.770
as a journalist and a scholar,

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.790
was not the mind of the killer,

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:50.318
which I wasn't really interested

00:06:50.318 --> 00:06:53.000
in spending time researching,
for obvious reasons.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.480
And, indeed, it wasn't even the 11

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.250
who had been killed, who I'm
sure were beautiful souls,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.250
each of whom might deserve
a book of their own.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.180
But what I kept coming back to,

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.310
given my own preexisting interest,

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.580
was the question of the neighborhood,

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.820
because I knew Squirrel Hill history

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.950
and I knew that it was,
arguably, the oldest,

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.990
most stable Jewish neighborhood

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:15.190
in American history,

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.373
which is something we
can talk about later.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.678
It had been substantively Jewish,

00:07:20.678 --> 00:07:22.540
about 1/3, give or take,

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:24.710
consistently for 100 years,

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.000
from the time of World War I

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.550
until the present, so a century exactly.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:29.520
And that's not something you could say

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.640
of any other neighborhood
in the United States.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:33.980
It had always been a neighborhood

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.520
relatively free of antisemitism,

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.570
even though Jews had been a minority.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:40.730
So it has always been Gentile majority,

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.740
and yet it's been a place
where Jews have felt safe.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:44.570
And Pittsburgh, indeed, has been a place

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:46.460
relatively free of antisemitism.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.890
So the irony that the
deadliest antisemitic attack

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.320
would come to Squirrel Hill

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:53.550
was not lost on me.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.100
And I got very, very interested

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.260
in how the neighborhood,

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:59.420
in part, the neighborhood's
religious congregations,

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:02.260
because there are many Jewish synagogues

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:04.900
in Squirrel Hill and also
other houses of worship,

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.430
but really the neighborhood more broadly

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:08.370
was going to cope.

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.020
What would it mean to a neighborhood?

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:13.460
So armed with this question,

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:14.520
a terrible metaphor, armed,

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.610
but with this question, let's say,

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:18.750
I began going to Pittsburgh

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:21.010
the month after the shooting, in November.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:22.980
Within a few weeks, I
had made my first trip,

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.880
and I ended up going 32 times

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:27.010
and interviewing about 250 people

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:28.340
over 18 months.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:29.790
And I stopped reporting

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:32.130
just as COVID was
descending across the land

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:32.963
in March of 2020,

00:08:32.963 --> 00:08:35.100
though that was entirely coincidental.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.500
I had planned for several months

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.350
that March would be
about my stopping point

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.530
because I'd be coming up on 18 months,

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.030
and I really had more
than enough interviews

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:45.360
and reporting already,
and I had planned already

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:46.730
to stop about then.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.450
So with these hundreds of interviews

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:51.670
and these several dozen trips,

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.190
I wrote this book that's really about

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:56.950
the concentric circles of people

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:58.380
affected by a tragedy,

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:01.750
that in the urb of a neighborhood

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.740
and in the kind of grid of life

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.593
of a neighborhood that's
walkable, bikeable,

00:09:06.593 --> 00:09:10.200
that has people naturally
colliding into each other

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.390
because of commerce, because
of religious worship,

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:14.040
because of public institutions

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.590
like schools and post office

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:19.390
and supermarkets and so forth and so on.

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:20.350
The public library, all of this,

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:21.960
Squirrel Hill has all these things.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:25.670
It's a kind of urban shtetl,
a kind of idyllic village.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:27.050
What would that mean

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.030
for how people reacted to grief?

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.300
And the book that I wrote
is a kind of patchwork

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:34.100
of portraits of the people

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:35.980
who become relevant

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:39.640
outside of the scope
of the national media,

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:41.180
and even sometimes outside the scope

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.310
of the archivists and historians who come,

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:45.390
and naturally and for good reason,

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:48.640
gravitate toward families of the victims,

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.060
the survivors, clergy people,

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.040
people who have a kind of official status.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.400
I was not as interested in people

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:55.890
with official status.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.960
So what I'd like to do now is read to you,

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.160
again, just two pages, and
then I wanna take you through

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.540
some photographs, and then
we'll read another bit more.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:04.280
The two pages that I wanna read to you

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:06.610
are from the prologue to the book.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:08.720
And it's where I learned the story

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.610
of a woman named Tammy Hepps,

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:12.840
who becomes something of a
through line through the book.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.420
She's a Harvard graduate,
about 40 years old

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:16.900
when this all happens.

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:18.020
Grew up outside Philadelphia,

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:19.010
in Cherry Hill, New Jersey,

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.150
but had migrated to Pittsburgh

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:22.300
in recent years

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:24.610
because she was an amateur genealogist

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.860
whose grandparents had
been from Homestead,

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:28.410
which is right across the river,

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:30.641
a steel town outside of Pittsburgh.

00:10:30.641 --> 00:10:32.350
And she was doing a lot
of genealogical research.

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:33.830
So she'd become very embedded

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:35.070
in Pittsburgh's Jewish community.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:36.120
She had moved to Squirrel Hill.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.423
She'd been there the
better part of a decade.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:41.810
And in the 24 hours after the killing,

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:43.640
really the 48 hours,

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:45.440
72 hours, she was everywhere.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:47.350
She was at the Jewish Community Center

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:49.990
consoling someone who had lost her husband

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:50.850
in the shooting.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.880
She was part of a group of
members of Bend the Arc,

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.600
a progressive Jewish organization,

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.890
that drafted a letter
asking that Donald Trump

00:10:57.890 --> 00:10:59.460
not come to visit.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.380
She was saying psalms, Tehillim,

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.602
outside of Tree of Life Synagogue

00:11:03.602 --> 00:11:05.050
while the bodies were still inside.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:07.150
Because a Jewish tradition
is that when keeping guard

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.700
over dead bodies, you recite psalms.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:12.930
She had done that, and
finally, completely wiped out,

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.900
she is trudging home

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:16.433
on Sunday afternoon.

00:11:17.353 --> 00:11:18.500
And this is what happens to her.

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:20.833
So have a listen for about three minutes.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:23.880
Just after 2:30,

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.450
she turned onto Murray Avenue

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:27.590
and a truck pulled up in front of her.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:28.670
On the side of it

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:30.860
were painted three curious words,

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:32.770
Crosses for Losses

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:34.130
As Tammy Hepps remembered it,

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.700
she looked into the truck
and saw a pile of crosses

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.370
in the back, Christian crosses.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:39.800
They were all white,

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:41.270
and on a quick count,

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.750
she decided there were 11 of them.

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:45.740
As soon as she grasped
what she was seeing,

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.847
she was incensed.

00:11:47.847 --> 00:11:49.777
"I thought to myself," Hepps recalled,

00:11:49.777 --> 00:11:52.490
"you have got to be kidding me."

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:54.820
And I looked around, and
no one else was there.

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:56.650
And I thought, if I have to be the one

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:58.990
to tell this man he can't put crosses

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:00.380
on the synagogue property,

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:01.880
I will be the one to tell him.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:05.110
Hepps had no idea who this guy was,

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:06.810
with this kind of nerve.

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.740
As she was figuring
out what to say to him,

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.270
trying to keep her cool,

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:13.050
she saw on the front seat of his truck

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:16.260
a pile of wooden six-pointed stars.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.690
She was relieved.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:19.060
I thought, okay,

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:21.340
what will happen is he'll
put the Stars of David

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:22.800
on the fronts of the crosses,

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:23.833
and it will be okay.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:28.110
Greg Zanis got out on the
driver's side of the truck

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.000
and approached Tammy Hepps.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.560
She looked him up and down.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:34.690
He was tired, unshaven, old.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:36.220
What was he doing here?

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:38.050
Where did he come from?

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.660
Then she looked down and saw his hands,

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.150
and it was as if something became clear.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:43.610
I saw his hands were covered

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:45.850
in white paint, Hepps remembered.

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:47.730
It's like he painted
these things overnight

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.700
and didn't even have
time to wash his hands.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:51.200
He told me his intention.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:53.127
He said to me, "I made these things,

00:12:53.127 --> 00:12:55.957
"got in a truck and drove nine hours.

00:12:55.957 --> 00:12:57.747
"I've been driving the whole time.

00:12:57.747 --> 00:13:00.227
"I don't even know the names
of the people who died.

00:13:00.227 --> 00:13:02.377
"I have to write their
names on the stars."

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:05.960
And then Hepps knew what she had to do.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.300
Her mother had emailed
her the names of the dead

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:11.630
just that morning, so she
had the names on her phone:

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.160
Joyce Fienberg, Richard Gottfried,

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:15.910
Rose Mallinger, Jerry Rabinowitz,

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:18.490
Cecil Rosenthal, David Rosenthal,

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:20.550
Bernice Simon, Sylvan Simon,

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:24.467
Daniel Stein, Melvin Wax, Irv Younger.

00:13:24.467 --> 00:13:26.927
"I brought up this list," she recalled,

00:13:26.927 --> 00:13:27.760
"and he said to me,

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:30.960
"'Can you please write the
names in my notebook?'"

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:32.760
And as he handed me a
pen and his notebook,

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:34.940
I was shaking as I copied the names down

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:36.740
so he could write them on the stars.

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:40.670
When Hepps had written down all 11 names,

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:42.800
she gave Zanis' notebook back to him.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.060
And now she had a question for him.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.107
I said to him, "Why do you do this?"

00:13:48.103 --> 00:13:49.380
And he said that there'd been gun violence

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:52.010
in his family, and this was his response.

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:53.997
He said, "You remember Parkland?

00:13:53.997 --> 00:13:55.357
"I did that one.

00:13:55.357 --> 00:13:56.877
"Remember Columbine?

00:13:56.877 --> 00:13:58.680
"I did that one, too."

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.720
It had never occurred
to me, Hepps told me,

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:03.260
that it was one person who had made it

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:06.060
his life's work to drive
around the country and do this.

00:14:06.919 --> 00:14:08.410
And at that moment, I realized that we

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.303
were just another one on the list.

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:14.170
So that's Tammy Hepps' encounter

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:16.600
with Greg Zanis, whose
nonprofit organization,

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:18.020
Crosses for Losses,

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:20.483
which was a nonprofit
of one, it was just him,

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:23.300
traveled around the country constantly,

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.530
putting up these white crosses

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.110
at the sites of mass tragedy.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.530
And again, that's just a kind of example

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:32.240
of the people who arrive and create,

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.670
who add color

00:14:35.622 --> 00:14:38.250
and create the effect and the mood

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.830
in the aftermath of a mass killing.

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:42.553
And I wanna show you some pictures now.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.840
So here, you can see the cover

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:48.780
of my book.

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:50.790
And it actually didn't occur to me

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:51.980
when I first saw it,

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:53.790
and it's a beautiful cover,

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:57.480
how symbolically interesting
it was if read as a text.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.210
What you see is the scene
outside Tree of Life

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.250
about 48 hours after the killing.

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:03.610
More than 48, 'cause it was the evening.

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:06.040
And you see that already,
it's become a shrine,

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:07.810
a memorial to the dead.

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:09.130
But what's interesting here is the way

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:10.570
that it's become an interfaith memorial,

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.630
because the candles that you see,

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:14.140
the little tea candles, are, of course,

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:16.070
a Jewish sign of mourning, right?

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:17.780
Jews light these candles on Yahrzeit

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:21.060
and on the anniversaries
of people's deaths.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.060
We, of course, also use, Jews, ritually,

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:26.250
to bring in the Sabbath, to
leave the Sabbath and so forth.

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.200
So candles are very liturgically Jewish.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:30.150
Flowers are not.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.440
Flowers, actually, are
a part of the American

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:34.540
and Christian idioms.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:36.300
And, of course, Christians
will bring flowers

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.360
to gravesides, which
Jews will typically not.

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:39.320
They'll bring candles

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:40.590
or they'll bring stones.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:41.760
We put stones on graves.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.230
But typically not flowers, except insofar

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:45.930
as in America, that's something people do.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.690
But already, you can see

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:50.070
that you have a kind of Jewish semiotics

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.930
and a Christian semiotics, tropes of both

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:56.210
of those communities coming together

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:57.470
in this very, very beautiful

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:59.190
makeshift kind of memorial garden

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:00.650
outside of Tree of Life

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:02.870
within just a couple days.

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:05.600
This is Greg Zanis, the
one I just talked about.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:07.640
He put millions of miles

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:09.100
on a whole series of pickup trucks

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:10.510
that he drove into the ground

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:13.780
from 1995 or six, when he first made

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.090
some crosses for a woman

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:19.910
in his grief support group
who had lost her son.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:21.420
Zanis had lost his father-in-law,

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:22.780
as I recall.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:23.997
And then Columbine
happened a few years later,

00:16:23.997 --> 00:16:25.710
and he started driving around the country,

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:27.790
and he went to every
mass killing he could.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:30.170
Also, he went to the sites
of natural disasters.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:32.346
He went to the sites of people

00:16:32.346 --> 00:16:34.596
who had been killed in
tornadoes, hurricanes,

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.563
huge traffic fatalities, things like that.

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:43.910
And he was an Evangelical
Christian himself,

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:45.950
but he always tried to
be culturally sensitive.

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:47.640
He would do things like
put the Star of David

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:49.030
on the front of the cross if he knew

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:50.350
that the victim was Jewish.

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:51.820
He also would put a crescent moon

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:53.510
if he knew that the victim was Muslim.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:55.390
He knew appropriate symbols for Sikhs

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:57.750
and Baha'i and Hindu and Shinto.

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:00.060
He even had a symbol,
and I forget what it was,

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.630
if he heard that the victim was an atheist

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:03.420
or an agnostic.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.487
So he was very, very sensitive,

00:17:05.487 --> 00:17:08.130
and he became a kind of one-man emissary

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:12.060
of interfaith deathcraft, if you will.

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:14.210
He himself died in December of 2020,

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:16.550
and you could find his obituary

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:18.200
in "The New York Times."

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:19.710
I was very interested in the way

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:22.840
that visual culture was
shaped by the aftermath

00:17:22.840 --> 00:17:24.370
of a mass killing.

00:17:24.370 --> 00:17:25.990
Everywhere you go,
whether it was the bombing

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:28.590
in Boston or whether it's
Parkland or whatever,

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:30.450
there ends up becoming
something that's this kind

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.250
of visual representation

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.460
by mutual mass agreement.

00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:36.870
And in Pittsburgh, it was this symbol,

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:39.430
which most of you have seen.

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:41.990
And it was created

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:44.590
by a graphic designer named Tim Hindes,

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.059
not spelled like Heinz
ketchup, which is, of course,

00:17:47.059 --> 00:17:48.590
one of the great Pittsburgh companies.

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:50.670
It's spelled H-I-N-D-E-S.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:54.690
And he was a lapsed Lutheran, Gentile,

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.120
German American graphic designer

00:17:57.120 --> 00:17:58.780
who lived in the south
suburbs of Pittsburgh.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:01.030
Didn't know anyone at Tree of Life.

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:03.470
Not kind of involved in interfaith work

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:04.303
or the Jewish community.

00:18:04.303 --> 00:18:08.920
Just a kind of Christian American dude

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:11.360
who wanted to do something to help.

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:13.010
And he's driving home,

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:15.311
listening to this on the radio.

00:18:15.311 --> 00:18:16.144
He happened to be in
Pittsburgh that morning

00:18:16.144 --> 00:18:17.133
when it happened.

00:18:18.032 --> 00:18:19.690
All the sudden, he fires up his Macintosh

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:22.720
and thinks, as a graphic
designer, what can I do?

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.270
And he wants to create
something that represents

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:28.450
the way that Pittsburgh embraces its Jews.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:29.920
So he thinks well, what's Pittsburgh?

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:31.997
And he thinks what's more Pittsburgh

00:18:31.997 --> 00:18:32.830
than the Pittsburgh Steelers?

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.330
So he takes the logo of
the Pittsburgh Steelers,

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:38.010
and then he replaces
the yellow hypocycloid

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:40.090
at the top, that's what
those shapes are called,

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:41.960
as I learned in reporting this book,

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:43.783
he replaces it with a yellow Star of David

00:18:43.783 --> 00:18:47.500
to kind of mash up the
Pittsburghery and the Jewry.

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:48.850
And then he replaces the word Steelers

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:50.240
with the words Stronger Than Hate,

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:52.640
playing on Boston Strong, Orlando Strong

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.290
and other terms you hear
and the catchphrases

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:57.050
in the aftermath of a mass killing.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:18:58.010
He puts it on Facebook.

00:18:58.010 --> 00:18:59.640
Within a few hours, it's
gone around the world

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:01.320
a trillion times or so.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:03.150
The next day at the Steelers game,

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:04.470
people are holding up posters

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:07.130
with this logo on them.

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:10.210
The Steelers end up
wearing it on their cleats.

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:11.960
You can find it on babies' onesies.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:13.800
You can find it on yarmulkes.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:16.170
And perhaps most movingly, it ends up

00:19:16.170 --> 00:19:18.960
being posted in the windows of storefronts

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:20.060
throughout Squirrel Hill,

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:23.150
all along the Forbes and
Murray commercial corridor.

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:25.640
And again, created by somebody not Jewish,

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:28.620
but lives 10 to 20 miles

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:31.350
from the site of the killing,

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:33.500
and it ends up kind of becoming

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.970
this Jewish, but also Jewish Pittsburgh,

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:39.280
interfaith, intercommunal symbol.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:41.060
Another one of those iconic symbols

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:43.030
was the headline of the
"Pittsburgh Post-Gazette"

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:45.020
on the Friday after the killings.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:47.500
Now, keep in mind, the 11 victims

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:49.573
who were from three
different congregations

00:19:49.573 --> 00:19:52.130
that were housed at the
Tree of Life building

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:53.710
were buried over four days.

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:54.990
So there were 11 funerals

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:56.970
over four days in a neighborhood

00:19:57.910 --> 00:19:59.380
less than three square miles.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:02.410
Constant crowds and traffic snarls

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:03.770
and people trying to
get from place to place.

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:06.170
And just a neighborhood
suffused with grief.

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:07.970
This headline ran on the last day

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:09.810
of the funerals, Friday morning.

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:11.610
And what's so interesting
is David Shribman,

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:13.480
now retired, but who
was the editor-in-chief

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:14.617
of the "Post-Gazette,"

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:19.660
he had this idea to run the Hebrew

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:21.280
for the first line of the Kaddish,

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.340
the mourner's prayer that Jews say,

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:24.439
the first line being

00:20:24.439 --> 00:20:26.110
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:28.643
hallowed and exalted be the name.

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:31.800
Be the great name, right?

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:35.190
And he didn't know how
to spell it in Hebrew.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:37.480
And he called his rabbi
at a different temple,

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:38.480
not Tree of Life.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:39.590
He's a member of Temple Sinai.

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:42.110
And Rabbi Gibson sent him the text

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:43.680
in an email.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.090
So Shribman then, as I learned,

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:47.220
took it to his graphic designer,

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:48.570
his chief designer at the "Post-Gazette,"

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:49.467
and said, "Make a headline out of this."

00:20:49.467 --> 00:20:51.377
And she said, "We actually don't have

00:20:51.377 --> 00:20:52.757
"a Hebrew font that big.

00:20:52.757 --> 00:20:54.187
"We don't have a Hebrew headline typeface

00:20:54.187 --> 00:20:55.440
"at the 'Post-Gazette.'"

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:57.010
So they had to kind of create it

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:58.810
from scratch right there.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:00.440
They got it up.

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:02.430
And the next day, Friday morning,

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:05.470
it hit people's doorsteps and newsstands,

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:07.130
and people just broke into tears.

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:09.010
It was so beautiful, so perfect,

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:11.330
so completely iconic.

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:12.680
Another one of those images

00:21:12.680 --> 00:21:15.050
that I think will
survive, this one forever,

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:17.560
quite literally, is the window

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:19.540
of the Starbucks at the
corner of Forbes and Shady,

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:21.520
the busier of the two Starbucks

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:22.710
in Squirrel Hill.

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:26.510
The lapsed Presbyterian Starbucks manager,

00:21:26.510 --> 00:21:28.950
Melissa Lysaght, wanted to do something

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:30.410
for her heavily Jewish clientele,

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:31.980
who she knew were grieving.

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:34.240
So she called her Roman Catholic friend

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:35.767
Nicole Flannery and said,

00:21:35.767 --> 00:21:37.610
"Can you do something
Jewish for the windows?"

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:39.590
Nicole Flannery's a local artist.

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:41.460
So Flannery connected

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.250
with an Orthodox Jewish man

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.500
and asked him for advice on what to do.

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:47.407
And he gave her the idea
for the Star of David,

00:21:47.407 --> 00:21:49.674
the tree life and the dove.

00:21:49.674 --> 00:21:51.479
And then he helped her with the words

00:21:51.479 --> 00:21:53.390
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:55.080
love, kindness and hope.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:57.210
And she painted this
over the next few days,

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:59.050
and it went up around Tuesday morning.

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:02.620
And it was like birds flying into windows.

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:04.070
(laughs) People just stopped,

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:06.290
or they just kept walking
and walked into the window.

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:07.630
They were so mesmerized,

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:09.680
they bumped into each
other on the sidewalk.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:11.740
And these are still there.

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:13.940
These windows, I think,
will be there forever.

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:14.843
And I just love the idea

00:22:14.843 --> 00:22:17.360
that 35 years from now,
there will be teenagers

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:19.140
inside that Starbucks,

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.657
looking at the reverse image and saying,

00:22:21.657 --> 00:22:24.047
"What the heck is that in the window?"

00:22:24.047 --> 00:22:25.322
They're sipping their Frappuccinos.

00:22:25.322 --> 00:22:26.550
They don't know about the shooting.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:29.480
And somebody, some old
hand inside this Starbucks

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:32.670
will explain to them why these words

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:35.660
and images are in the Starbucks window.

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:38.450
I took this photograph,
which is why it's terrible.

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.500
I'm a poor photographer.

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:40.630
Composition is bad.

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:43.090
This is the sign off the highway

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:44.790
as you drive up Forward Avenue

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:47.190
to the base of Murray,
and enter Squirrel Hill.

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:48.970
And if you look at the H in Squirrel Hill,

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:52.210
you'll see a little Star of
David is hanging from it,

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:55.490
a little glitter paper Star of David.

00:22:55.490 --> 00:22:57.940
This is one of thousands
of Stars of David,

00:22:57.940 --> 00:23:00.120
very small, a few inches across,

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:02.780
that had been handmade and collected

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:04.490
from around the world.

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:06.200
Some were made of Popsicle sticks.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:07.620
Some were made as school projects

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:10.200
out of cut paper.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:13.080
Some were knit or crocheted

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:16.310
or macramed or papier-mached, you name it.

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:18.320
And they were collected
through a Facebook group

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:20.920
that then, in November,
went around and hung them

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:23.120
all over the city, from signs,

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:26.220
from statuary, from trees.

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:28.140
And most of them didn't last the winter

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:29.540
because of the weather, but, of course,

00:23:29.540 --> 00:23:31.970
in the winter, you
could look up sometimes,

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:34.230
if you were on Forbes Avenue, let's say,

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.480
and there'd be a light snow.

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:39.850
And the street lamps

00:23:39.850 --> 00:23:41.320
would be reflecting off the snow

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:43.860
and then off of these stars.

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:45.940
And it was as if the
stars were representing

00:23:45.940 --> 00:23:48.300
the dead, as if there
were thousands of angels

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:50.250
in the sky above the streets.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:51.260
It was just so beautiful.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:52.820
This was the last one I saw.

00:23:52.820 --> 00:23:54.590
In March, it was still hanging there.

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:57.260
And I would always check
when I was driving into town,

00:23:57.260 --> 00:23:59.540
is that star still there?

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:00.720
And then the next week

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:01.870
after I took this photo, it wasn't there.

00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:03.460
It was on the ground in the mud.

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:04.977
And I found it. I have it.

00:24:04.977 --> 00:24:07.810
But it was kind of a
worldwide project, right?

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:09.100
So all of a sudden, there's a kind

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:12.080
of homely Hamish public art

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:13.093
created around the world

00:24:13.093 --> 00:24:14.780
that ends up decorating

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:16.950
Squirrel Hill in Pittsburgh,

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:18.327
which I just think is a
kind of beautiful effect

00:24:18.327 --> 00:24:20.663
and very, very, very contemporary.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:22.500
I include this photograph just because

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:26.020
it's important to note that
there were Orthodox Jews

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:27.770
walking with the hearse

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:30.863
of one of the victims on
the way to the interment.

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:33.330
None of the victims was Orthodox,

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:35.010
and yet a lot of the people who helped

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:36.880
with the grieving
process, in various ways,

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:39.350
acting as shomrim, for example, as guards

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:42.530
over the bodies, in some
cases cleansing the bodies,

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:44.640
performing the ritual of Tahara,

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:46.520
were Orthodox, and that kind

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.720
of not just interreligious cooperation,

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:51.850
but intrareligious
cooperation, is quite rare.

00:24:51.850 --> 00:24:53.450
One of the interesting things I found

00:24:53.450 --> 00:24:56.750
is that Squirrel Hill
has a very unusual level

00:24:56.750 --> 00:24:59.590
of amity amongst the different
Jewish denominations,

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:01.180
which is something

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.283
that really does bear further study.

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:06.550
Another thing that happens
after a mass killing

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:08.800
is a lot of money comes in.

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:09.730
Some of it is raised

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:12.180
by quasi-official organizations,

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:14.620
in this case, the Jewish
Federation of Greater Pittsburgh,

00:25:14.620 --> 00:25:16.900
but some of it is raised
by solo operators.

00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:21.480
This is Shay Khatiri, an
ex-Muslim Iranian expatriate

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:24.100
now living inside the
Beltway down in Washington,

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:26.130
doing a master's at one

00:25:26.130 --> 00:25:28.853
of the Johns Hopkins programs in DC.

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:31.440
He woke up that morning.

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:32.882
He heard what had happened.

00:25:32.882 --> 00:25:34.310
He was sick to his stomach.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:35.810
He went to a local coffee shop

00:25:35.810 --> 00:25:39.000
and opened up a gofundme.org page

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:40.330
to raise money.

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:42.210
And within a few days,

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:44.110
it had raised $1.5 million

00:25:44.110 --> 00:25:46.230
for the Jews of Squirrel,

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:48.140
well, for Tree of Life

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:50.590
and the victims' families and so forth,

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:52.640
for the congregations and the families,

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:54.383
making him, I think,

00:25:55.835 --> 00:25:58.620
probably the largest single fundraiser,

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.380
non-Jewish fundraiser,

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:03.470
for the Jewish community
of Squirrel Hill ever.

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:05.120
When I asked him to
provide a photo of himself,

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:06.460
he sent me this picture of himself

00:26:06.460 --> 00:26:07.630
standing in front of the White House

00:26:07.630 --> 00:26:08.860
holding Plato's "Republic,"

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:11.280
which I think tells you
about how he sees himself

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:13.730
and his commitment to
Western civilization,

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:16.880
and wanting to be part
of the American project.

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:18.060
A group of teenagers,

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:19.990
seniors at Taylor Allderdice High School,

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:21.710
the big public high
school in Squirrel Hill,

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:23.660
gathered at that Starbucks to organize

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:24.660
a vigil that night.

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:26.290
It happened six or seven hours

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:29.590
after the crime scene was secured.

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:32.830
And it was a Havdalah ritual,

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:34.390
the ritual that brings out the Sabbath

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:36.250
that ends Saturday.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:37.930
And here you could see Emily Pressman

00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:39.850
holding the Havdalah candle,

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:42.120
an observant Jewish girl, woman.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:43.290
She's in college now.

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:44.950
They were high school seniors at the time.

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:47.100
To the right of her is Isabel Smith,

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:50.653
who was a classmate of hers at Allderdice.

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:54.610
African American, biracial,
ancestrally Christian,

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:56.830
though she told me she's
not particularly religious.

00:26:56.830 --> 00:26:59.500
And it gives you a sense
of the interfaith nature,

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:00.920
again, of the healing.

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:03.580
What these girls did,
along with some boys,

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:06.573
was pull out several thousand people

00:27:06.573 --> 00:27:08.530
to the heart of the Squirrel
Hill commercial district

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:09.440
within a few hours.

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:10.880
They had planned the whole thing.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:13.320
They got stages, they got a
guitarist, they got candles,

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:16.260
they got amplification system, everything.

00:27:16.260 --> 00:27:19.450
And here at six or seven p.m., at dusk,

00:27:19.450 --> 00:27:22.020
you see the crowds

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:24.090
at the corners of Forbes and Murray.

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:26.104
And what's important,

00:27:26.104 --> 00:27:27.120
you could see their umbrellas out.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.500
That day, there was rain off and on.

00:27:29.500 --> 00:27:30.950
What's really interesting

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:31.970
is what you don't see.

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:33.700
The photograph is taken

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:36.510
from the steps of Sixth
Presbyterian Church,

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:38.240
which, for 40 years,

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:41.580
was the church of Mister
Rogers, Fred Rogers.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:44.140
As all good Pittsburghers
know, Mister Rogers

00:27:44.140 --> 00:27:45.620
was a Pittsburgher.

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:47.070
He taped the show

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:50.960
at the public TV station

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:53.306
just down the road from Squirrel Hill.

00:27:53.306 --> 00:27:54.860
Actually, I actually think
it's in Squirrel Hill.

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:57.618
It's technically not the
Oakland neighborhood yet.

00:27:57.618 --> 00:27:58.451
There's a lotta controversy over that.

00:27:58.451 --> 00:27:59.770
People will typically call it Oakland,

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:01.600
but I think it's still in Squirrel Hill.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:04.620
And Mister Rogers was one
of the great local allies

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:05.480
to the Jews.

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:06.610
A religious Presbyterian,

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:08.460
an ordained minister, in fact, who,

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:10.160
when he set out to create

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:12.400
a fictional neighborhood,
"Mister Rogers' Neighborhood,"

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:14.950
that represented a world in which children

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:16.640
could play and feel safe

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:18.560
and give free rein to their imagination,

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:20.900
he looked out the window of his house

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:22.110
and what he saw was Squirrel Hill,

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:23.400
which is where he lived.

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:26.100
And so literally, this is
Mister Rogers' neighborhood,

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.900
and this photograph is
taken from the steps

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:28.753
of his church.

00:28:30.450 --> 00:28:31.300
The president came.

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:32.910
Politicians do tend to come

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:36.085
to mass killings, if
they are deadly enough,

00:28:36.085 --> 00:28:38.700
(laughs) which is a
rather macabre discussion.

00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:41.350
At what point do you qualify
for a presidential visit?

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:42.730
Not at four or five dead,

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:44.330
but certainly at 11 dead.

00:28:44.330 --> 00:28:45.530
Some people, as I discussed,

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:47.170
didn't want President Trump to come,

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:49.300
but ultimately, Rabbi
Myers of Tree of Life

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:51.350
felt that it was his
job to offer hospitality

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:53.550
to anyone who came, and he welcomed

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:54.850
the president and first lady,

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:56.250
and talked to them for about 20 minutes

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:57.640
inside the building.

00:28:57.640 --> 00:28:58.820
Here they are outside the building.

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:01.533
And you can see at waist level,

00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:04.560
Greg Zanis' Crosses for Losses

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:06.120
with the Stars of David

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:07.207
and people's names on the front.

00:29:07.207 --> 00:29:09.480
You can see Sylvan Simon, Bernice Simon,

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:11.380
Daniel Stein and Melvin Wax

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:14.300
all inscribed on those stars.

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:15.840
There was an anti-Trump protest.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:17.200
Thousands of people attended that.

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:18.620
In fact, somebody said to me

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:20.800
they thought that the anti-Trump protest

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:22.940
was the single largest gathering of Jews

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:25.210
in one place in Pittsburgh history,

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:26.560
which is probably true.

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:28.910
It was probably more Jews
than ever had gathered

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:30.780
in one synagogue building

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:33.550
for Rosh Hashanah or Yom
Kippur or anything else.

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:34.453
It was massive.

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:37.500
To express their anger at Trump,

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.320
they performed a kind of makeshift

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:42.680
keriah ritual.

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:44.890
Those of you who know the
rituals of Jewish mourning

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:47.306
know that you often rend a garment

00:29:47.306 --> 00:29:48.730
when you hear that a loved one has died.

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:51.740
And in modernity, instead
of rending the garment,

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:53.900
often people will affix a black ribbon

00:29:53.900 --> 00:29:56.390
to their outfit, and then
tear the black ribbon.

00:29:56.390 --> 00:29:59.417
So to express their
grief and anger at Trump,

00:29:59.417 --> 00:30:01.760
what they did, what the organizers did,

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:05.720
was hand out thousands
of pieces of black paper.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:06.553
And then when they gave the signal,

00:30:06.553 --> 00:30:08.610
everyone went quiet and
held the black paper

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:10.680
up to the sky and tore it all at once.

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:12.250
So you just heard the tearing,

00:30:12.250 --> 00:30:15.030
as if there were a rend in
the fabric of the universe

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:17.030
at the anti-Trump protest.

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:18.783
Now, not everyone was so subtle.

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:22.140
There were some angrier gestures,

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:23.620
but those were pretty rare.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:25.420
And, in fact, the protest

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:26.600
was overwhelmingly peaceful.

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:27.916
There was one arrest,

00:30:27.916 --> 00:30:28.770
and it was an arrest of someone

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:30.130
who was being quite nonviolent.

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:31.650
This is University of Pittsburgh

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:33.930
sociology professor Joshua Bloom,

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:36.610
the only person arrested at the protest.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:38.820
He sat down in front of Trump's motorcade

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:40.650
and began meditating and chanting

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:42.050
and praying and singing.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:43.500
And the police took him away.

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:45.120
He emphasized to me when I interviewed him

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:47.410
that the police were
quite gentle with him,

00:30:47.410 --> 00:30:48.890
and he was released the next day

00:30:48.890 --> 00:30:50.513
and no charges were pressed.

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:53.670
Celebrities come in the
aftermath of a mass killing.

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:56.600
Here you can see another
president and first lady.

00:30:56.600 --> 00:30:57.440
That's Tom Hanks,

00:30:57.440 --> 00:31:00.320
perhaps the most popular American ever.

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:03.340
He has his arm around Mister
Rogers' widow, Joanne Rogers.

00:31:03.340 --> 00:31:05.800
This is at a memorial for the dead,

00:31:05.800 --> 00:31:07.410
I believe in December, a couple months

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:08.833
after the killing.

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:11.603
Now, this is interesting because,

00:31:12.760 --> 00:31:14.210
those of you who are football fans

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:17.230
will recognize that
lustrous head of white hair.

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:18.590
It's Robert Kraft, the owner

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:19.880
of the New England Patriots.

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:21.890
And one of the things that
happens after a mass killing

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:23.340
is when people come to town,

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:24.580
they wanna pay their respect.

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:26.320
So Robert Kraft, who grew up

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:28.040
in an observant Jewish home,

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:30.480
did come to worship with Tree of Life

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:32.330
the Saturday before the Sunday

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:35.300
on which his Patriots
were to play the Steelers.

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:37.130
Tree of Life was, by the way, not in use,

00:31:37.130 --> 00:31:38.540
and still isn't in use.

00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:40.310
The congregations are
all worshiping elsewhere.

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:42.800
So this came after services

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:45.170
at Rodef Shalom Temple,

00:31:45.170 --> 00:31:46.940
which is where Tree of Life has relocated

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:48.490
and rented space there.

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:49.660
But what's interesting, of course,

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:51.750
is you'll notice that the owner
of the New England Patriots

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:53.740
is wearing the Pittsburgh Steelers

00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:55.080
Stronger Than Hate yarmulke.

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:56.840
And it struck me, this is the first time

00:31:56.840 --> 00:31:58.520
probably in world history

00:31:58.520 --> 00:32:01.300
that the owner of one AFC NFL team

00:32:01.300 --> 00:32:04.090
has worn the garb of a rival team,

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:05.920
but he did it, for obvious reasons.

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:09.490
He was in the house of the Steelers,

00:32:09.490 --> 00:32:10.430
and he put it on.

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:12.160
And although I don't
normally take photographs

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:13.790
on the Sabbath, I felt like God

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:15.200
wanted me to take out my iPhone

00:32:15.200 --> 00:32:18.240
and surreptitiously snap
a photo of Robert Kraft,

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:20.293
Patriots owner, wearing Steelers garb.

00:32:21.730 --> 00:32:23.950
Funny stuff in the
aftermath of a mass killing.

00:32:23.950 --> 00:32:26.890
Dogs come from hundreds of miles away,

00:32:26.890 --> 00:32:28.130
if not more.

00:32:28.130 --> 00:32:29.610
Therapy dogs, support dogs.

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:31.000
People who believe that their animals

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:32.160
will help people heal.

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:33.790
I talked to one woman from New Jersey

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:35.960
who brought a bunch of dogs

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:38.340
many, many hours to Pittsburgh.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:39.670
I don't know whose dogs these were,

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:41.370
but you see them wearing the red vest

00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:43.550
that indicate they're some
sort of support animals.

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:45.960
And you see this girl petting the dogs

00:32:45.960 --> 00:32:47.890
and holding a note she had written

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:49.200
for the local police.

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:51.590
If you squint, you can
see that the note says

00:32:51.590 --> 00:32:52.930
thank you for keeping the Jews

00:32:52.930 --> 00:32:54.380
in my neighborhood safe.

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:56.493
Lily, age six.

00:32:57.490 --> 00:32:59.810
I'm a dog person, and I choke up

00:32:59.810 --> 00:33:02.260
every time I see this photograph.

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:04.410
And finally, I just wanna show you

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:06.770
a picture of the charming

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:08.230
and handsome Robert Zacharias,

00:33:08.230 --> 00:33:09.450
who became a friend of mine,

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:11.692
holding out a challah that he made.

00:33:11.692 --> 00:33:12.910
The picture was taken by
his girlfriend, Molly.

00:33:12.910 --> 00:33:15.470
They are lovely people whose company

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:16.573
I really got to enjoy.

00:33:18.033 --> 00:33:20.860
And I wanna tell you something
about Robert Zacharias.

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:22.460
So just take a look at him,

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:24.150
take a look at his beautiful punim

00:33:25.196 --> 00:33:27.080
and his nice smile and countenance.

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:28.500
And then I wanna read you

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.050
a bit from the epilogue of my book.

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:32.510
I wanna conclude by reading to you

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:37.173
this bit about Robert Zacharias,

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:40.870
who changed something about himself

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:43.123
as a result of the killing.

00:33:43.967 --> 00:33:45.673
And in the next three minutes,
you're gonna learn why.

00:33:46.785 --> 00:33:49.420
The morning of the shooting,
Zach, as he is known,

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:51.650
had been hanging with a
friend at her apartment

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:52.750
in the Bloomfield neighborhood,

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:54.410
northwest of Squirrel Hill.

00:33:54.410 --> 00:33:56.880
After hearing about the
killing, Zach went home.

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:58.860
He checked in with his family and friends,

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:01.100
and a couple hours
later, in mid-afternoon,

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:03.200
he went to the Giant Eagle supermarket

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:05.170
to buy soup and Yahrzeit candles,

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:08.113
small candles one lights
to honor the dead.

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:10.600
Before he left,

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:13.360
he dug into his closet
for one of the yarmulkes.

00:34:13.360 --> 00:34:15.600
He put it on and left for the market.

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:17.610
It was the first time
he'd ever worn a yarmulke

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:19.520
when it wasn't Shabbat or a Jewish holiday

00:34:19.520 --> 00:34:21.660
or some other special occasion.

00:34:21.660 --> 00:34:24.470
He kept the yarmulke on his
head at the Havdalah event

00:34:24.470 --> 00:34:26.490
planned by the high school students,

00:34:26.490 --> 00:34:28.440
and he has been wearing one ever since.

00:34:29.287 --> 00:34:30.687
"It is a new experience for me

00:34:30.687 --> 00:34:31.627
"to be wearing a yarmulke

00:34:31.627 --> 00:34:33.560
"out in the world on a regular basis,"

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:34.737
Zach said to me.

00:34:34.737 --> 00:34:37.530
"It has these interesting implications."

00:34:37.530 --> 00:34:39.480
He mentioned a night when
he and his girlfriend

00:34:39.480 --> 00:34:42.530
had been trying to decide
where to go out and eat.

00:34:42.530 --> 00:34:45.030
I was like, "Well, Gooski's is open."

00:34:45.030 --> 00:34:45.863
And then I thought,

00:34:45.863 --> 00:34:48.873
wait, I heard they have
some Nazis at Gooski's.

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:51.630
Zach was referring to the rumor that,

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:54.590
at some point, some sort
of Neo-Nazi punk band

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:56.750
had played a gig at Gooski's,

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:58.990
a little dive bar in their
Polish Hill neighborhood.

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:01.840
Zach figured that the
rumor was probably false,

00:35:01.840 --> 00:35:04.360
but his head was spinning
with possibility.

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:07.030
He thought about the
overlap between punks,

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:09.250
some of whom lived in Polish Hill,

00:35:09.250 --> 00:35:12.820
and various strains of
white nationalist ideology.

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:15.390
And he realized that
if there were neo-Nazis

00:35:15.390 --> 00:35:16.850
hanging out at Gooski's,

00:35:16.850 --> 00:35:18.323
he now looked like a target.

00:35:19.637 --> 00:35:21.157
"But I didn't wanna take my yarmulke off

00:35:21.157 --> 00:35:23.477
"to go to Gooski's and
have dinner," he said.

00:35:23.477 --> 00:35:24.853
"That didn't feel right.

00:35:25.742 --> 00:35:27.927
"That's some kind of
bizarre paying of obeisance

00:35:27.927 --> 00:35:29.817
"to the ghost or not ghost Nazis,

00:35:29.817 --> 00:35:32.237
"and I don't wanna do that,
but I also don't wanna not go

00:35:32.237 --> 00:35:34.207
"to Gooski's if I wanna go
to some neighborhood bar

00:35:34.207 --> 00:35:35.797
"that has vegan kielbasa."

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.980
The yarmulke, Zach realized,
complicated everything.

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:41.940
If he took it off, the neo-Nazis,

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:43.840
real or imagined, would win.

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:46.000
If he kept it on, but skipped Gooski's,

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:48.360
the neo-Nazis, real or imagined,

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:51.470
would have deprived him
of tasty vegan kielbasa.

00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:54.253
But if he said yes to the
yarmulke and to Gooski's,

00:35:54.253 --> 00:35:56.660
then he might be putting
his visibly Jewish self

00:35:56.660 --> 00:36:00.110
in sight of the real
or imaginary neo-Nazis.

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:02.170
Now, to be clear, there is no evidence,

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:04.420
actually, that neo-Nazis
ever hung out at Gooski's.

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:07.170
I did the research on
this, pure urban legend.

00:36:07.170 --> 00:36:08.940
Anyway, Zach weighed his options,

00:36:08.940 --> 00:36:10.350
screwed up his courage and took himself

00:36:10.350 --> 00:36:11.183
and his girlfriend to Gooski's.

00:36:11.183 --> 00:36:12.930
And when he stepped inside,

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:14.350
he took a look around and thought,

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:16.750
these aren't antisemites here.

00:36:16.750 --> 00:36:18.540
He saw a co-worker of his at the bar

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:20.950
reading "The New York
Times" app on his iPhone

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:22.980
as he drank a beer and smoked a cigarette.

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:25.530
It was all copacetic at Gooski's.

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:28.370
But the yarmulke was not
finished with him yet.

00:36:28.370 --> 00:36:29.203
He and his girlfriend sat at the bar

00:36:29.203 --> 00:36:31.497
and placed their orders.

00:36:31.497 --> 00:36:33.920
"I made a very expensive
order by mistake,"

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.347
Zach told me, "and then I was like,

00:36:35.347 --> 00:36:37.400
"oh, I shouldn't get that."

00:36:37.400 --> 00:36:39.090
He turned away from the
bar to look his menu,

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:41.440
to figure out what to order instead.

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:42.480
But as he turned away,

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:44.530
he noticed the bartender
looking at his yarmulke,

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:46.730
and then he was immediately concerned

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:49.210
that she would see him changing his order

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.400
as a manifestation of his Jewishness.

00:36:51.400 --> 00:36:53.927
I was like, "Oh, God damn it.

00:36:53.927 --> 00:36:55.687
"Now I'm a money-grubbing Jew.

00:36:55.687 --> 00:36:58.017
"Like, I'm complaining
about the expensive order

00:36:58.017 --> 00:37:00.287
"and adjusting my order
to be less expensive.

00:37:00.287 --> 00:37:01.657
"So I felt bad about that

00:37:01.657 --> 00:37:03.037
"because now I'm some sort of symbol

00:37:03.037 --> 00:37:04.867
"of all the Jews.

00:37:04.867 --> 00:37:06.827
"I'm the standard-bearer
for all these people

00:37:06.827 --> 00:37:09.773
"whom I don't bear a
standard for," he recalled.

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:13.140
Zach was still feeling
like the guy in a yarmulke

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:14.880
who had flagged a problem with his tab

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:17.770
when something happened
to compound his misery.

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:20.070
He lifted his bottle of
Yuengling beer to his mouth

00:37:20.070 --> 00:37:22.410
to have a sip, and a bit sloshed out

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:25.440
and landed on a man sitting
next to him at the bar.

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:26.820
It was just a tiny bit of beer,

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:28.730
and it seemed the man didn't notice,

00:37:28.730 --> 00:37:31.050
so Zach elected not to say anything.

00:37:31.050 --> 00:37:33.680
I thought, oh, he doesn't
notice, doesn't matter.

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:35.640
But no sooner had Zach elected

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:37.030
to let the matter drop

00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:38.210
than the man turned around.

00:37:38.210 --> 00:37:39.650
He had noticed.

00:37:39.650 --> 00:37:41.230
Zach quickly apologized,

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:43.320
tried to make everything
right, but it was too late.

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:44.387
I was like, "I'm sorry.

00:37:44.387 --> 00:37:45.690
"I spilled some beer,"

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:48.020
but I hadn't said it
when I spilled the beer.

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:50.180
So yet again, I think, now I'm a jerk,

00:37:50.180 --> 00:37:52.300
a selfish, unapologetic Jew person.

00:37:52.300 --> 00:37:54.640
So it's even worse. Everything is bad.

00:37:54.640 --> 00:37:57.270
Zach threw his hands up in despair.

00:37:57.270 --> 00:37:59.673
It seems that a public Jew can never win.

00:38:00.600 --> 00:38:01.940
So I like ending on that one

00:38:01.940 --> 00:38:04.020
because it sort of tells you

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:05.700
how far-reaching the ramifications

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:07.130
of a mass killing are, right?

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:09.620
11 people go to synagogue on Shabbat

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:11.070
to three different congregations

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:12.243
inside one building.

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:15.530
A white nationalist antisemite,

00:38:15.530 --> 00:38:17.720
fueled principally by
anti-immigrant sentiment,

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:19.110
and we can talk about his motivations,

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:21.770
if you want, enters the building

00:38:21.770 --> 00:38:24.770
and murders 11 people
and wounds two others,

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:27.543
and then wounds several
of the police who respond.

00:38:28.980 --> 00:38:30.190
And then as you look at what happens

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:32.140
over the next day and the next week

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:34.240
of sitting shiva and
the month of Sheloshim

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:39.080
and the whole year of
mourning, you discover

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:42.810
that dogs come and a medical clown

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:45.760
from Israel comes to clown for people.

00:38:45.760 --> 00:38:48.180
And a guy living a
neighborhood or two over

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:49.013
starts wearing a yarmulke.

00:38:49.013 --> 00:38:50.770
And a woman named Lynn Hyde

00:38:50.770 --> 00:38:52.650
living in the downtown district

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:54.930
hears the sirens go by

00:38:54.930 --> 00:38:56.487
on the way to Tree of Life.

00:38:56.487 --> 00:38:58.080
And when she finds out what happens,

00:38:58.080 --> 00:38:59.400
she makes the decision, finally,

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:00.560
to convert to Judaism,

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:02.750
something she'd been
thinking about for years.

00:39:02.750 --> 00:39:05.040
And an African American
woman named Rose McGee,

00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:06.980
living outside of Minneapolis,

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:08.300
decides to bake pies

00:39:08.300 --> 00:39:11.363
for the victims' families
and the congregations.

00:39:12.230 --> 00:39:14.010
And then thinks, I believe the Jews

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:18.730
have some dietary laws,
and she ends up asking

00:39:18.730 --> 00:39:20.760
the local Jewish day school,
the Jewish elementary school,

00:39:20.760 --> 00:39:23.860
can I use your kitchen to
bake my sweet potato pies?

00:39:23.860 --> 00:39:25.750
Because I want them to be kosher,

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:28.060
and I understand I need
to use a special kitchen.

00:39:28.060 --> 00:39:30.100
And then ends up baking the pies there,

00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:31.750
the first kosher pies she's ever made,

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:33.720
and then flying them to Pittsburgh

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:34.860
to give them to people.

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:38.210
You end up seeing these sort
of endless concentric circles

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:40.853
of helping and healing and loving.

00:39:41.820 --> 00:39:44.700
And what I argue implicitly in this book

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:46.910
is that those end up happening much more

00:39:46.910 --> 00:39:48.500
it they are facilitated

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:49.830
by a certain kind of neighborhood,

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:53.180
a neighborhood that is
both geographically dense,

00:39:53.180 --> 00:39:55.820
diverse and pluralistic,
not just between Jews

00:39:55.820 --> 00:39:58.240
and non-Jews, but of
different kinds of Jews,

00:39:58.240 --> 00:40:00.600
that has many, many
multi-generational families.

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:02.860
I kept meeting people who'd
grown up in Squirrel Hill,

00:40:02.860 --> 00:40:04.217
left, and then moved back there.

00:40:04.217 --> 00:40:05.380
People who were third-

00:40:05.380 --> 00:40:07.380
and fourth-generation Squirrel Hill people

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:08.360
and so forth, so on.

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:10.370
That basically everything about

00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:12.170
the urbanist structure

00:40:12.170 --> 00:40:15.390
of this specific
neighborhood offered lessons

00:40:15.390 --> 00:40:19.120
about how you could
imagine a community that,

00:40:19.120 --> 00:40:20.970
should something terrible happen,

00:40:20.970 --> 00:40:23.150
would not only be able to survive,

00:40:23.150 --> 00:40:25.620
but, in some senses, to thrive,

00:40:25.620 --> 00:40:27.510
and that's what the book is about.

00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:28.773
So thank you very much.

00:40:32.390 --> 00:40:33.880
- Wow, Mark, thank you so much.

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:36.040
That was just really great.

00:40:36.040 --> 00:40:38.660
And you have such

00:40:40.308 --> 00:40:42.880
an amazing talent to
capture people's words,

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:45.600
and even your performance of them

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:47.547
was just really
- Thank you.

00:40:47.547 --> 00:40:49.500
- Spectacular, so thank you.

00:40:49.500 --> 00:40:51.070
So a bunch of questions.

00:40:51.070 --> 00:40:54.670
And I urge everybody to please use

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:56.040
the Q and A function

00:40:56.930 --> 00:40:58.793
at the bottom of your screen.

00:40:59.910 --> 00:41:00.770
So

00:41:02.190 --> 00:41:04.450
a few questions that are coming in,

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:06.630
just to say more about,

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:08.643
and I'll maybe ask you a few,

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.090
a couple questions
together and let you riff.

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:14.530
- Sure.
- More about the neighborhood.

00:41:14.530 --> 00:41:17.220
So about the specificity

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:18.150
of the neighborhood.

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:20.540
How, you kind of referred
to this at the beginning,

00:41:20.540 --> 00:41:23.970
just unusual Squirrel Hill is

00:41:23.970 --> 00:41:24.803
as a neighborhood.

00:41:24.803 --> 00:41:27.680
And one of my colleagues

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:29.410
points out that she lived

00:41:29.410 --> 00:41:32.870
in the Squirrel Hill area as a postdoc

00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:34.520
in the 1990s.

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:37.720
That the Jewish presence
was really noticeable,

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:40.850
as was, she remembers, a WPA mural

00:41:40.850 --> 00:41:43.970
from the Depression in the post office

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:45.650
of Squirrel Hill

00:41:45.650 --> 00:41:47.453
that shows a rabbi.

00:41:49.022 --> 00:41:52.070
And how struck she was,
and how unusual is that,

00:41:52.070 --> 00:41:55.387
to actually have in a public civil

00:41:55.387 --> 00:41:56.780
(Mark laughs)
building, a kind

00:41:56.780 --> 00:41:59.390
of representation of Jewishness like that.

00:41:59.390 --> 00:42:00.620
- Yeah, well, thank you for that.

00:42:00.620 --> 00:42:01.810
I have a number of thoughts.

00:42:01.810 --> 00:42:04.503
First of all, I actually never,

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:07.150
I either never went in and saw

00:42:07.150 --> 00:42:09.370
or never noticed or
took note of that mural.

00:42:09.370 --> 00:42:11.500
There are a lot of WPM
murals in post offices.

00:42:11.500 --> 00:42:12.910
I'm sure this has been written about.

00:42:12.910 --> 00:42:14.250
Where I live in New Haven, in Westville,

00:42:14.250 --> 00:42:16.340
we have a WPA mural in our post office.

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:18.140
And one of the reasons
why we periodically fight

00:42:18.140 --> 00:42:19.430
to save the post office,

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:21.280
when they decide to try to save money

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:22.390
by shutting it down, is because

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:25.780
it is so artistically
significant, I think.

00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:28.580
- Here, I can share, actually, my screen.

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:31.149
She actually helpfully sent it.

00:42:31.149 --> 00:42:33.185
So let me see-
- Fabulous.

00:42:33.185 --> 00:42:34.240
- [Maurice] Work for one second.

00:42:34.240 --> 00:42:35.180
- It would not be surprising

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:36.810
that in the '30s already,

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:38.110
that there would have been

00:42:39.140 --> 00:42:40.650
people taking note of the Jewish presence

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:41.580
in Squirrel Hill because,

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:43.170
as I said from the beginning,

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:46.290
it was a very religiously
integrated community

00:42:46.290 --> 00:42:48.163
with a strong Jewish minority.

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:51.610
The Jews at that point were typically

00:42:51.610 --> 00:42:53.990
either in Allegheny City or
they were in East Liberty.

00:42:53.990 --> 00:42:55.600
They were in other neighborhoods.

00:42:55.600 --> 00:42:57.040
And Pittsburgh was spreading east,

00:42:57.040 --> 00:43:00.030
and Squirrel Hill was one
of the easternmost outposts.

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:01.380
And it was hilly and it was nice.

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:03.480
It initially had been very tough to settle

00:43:04.638 --> 00:43:06.540
because it's a hill, and the trains

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:08.640
that would carry coal and
other kind of commodities

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:10.890
that a neighborhood needs, initially,

00:43:10.890 --> 00:43:13.150
the streetcars couldn't make it up

00:43:13.150 --> 00:43:14.380
all the hills of Squirrel Hill.

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:16.730
And so you needed some
technological advances

00:43:16.730 --> 00:43:18.840
before it could be well-settled.

00:43:18.840 --> 00:43:21.200
And then in the late, I think in the 1890s

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:23.770
or around 1900, they
started to develop it.

00:43:23.770 --> 00:43:26.380
It was land purchased
by Gentile developers.

00:43:26.380 --> 00:43:28.940
It was not as if Jews
said, "Let's settle here,"

00:43:28.940 --> 00:43:32.253
but it quickly became popular with Jews,

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:37.120
and has stayed that way.

00:43:37.120 --> 00:43:38.690
Another note that I wanna make

00:43:38.690 --> 00:43:40.580
before I answer the question

00:43:40.580 --> 00:43:42.090
a bit more substantively

00:43:42.090 --> 00:43:43.530
is that everywhere I go,

00:43:43.530 --> 00:43:45.010
I now have this magical superpower

00:43:45.010 --> 00:43:46.910
of bringing out ex-Pittsburghers,

00:43:46.910 --> 00:43:48.680
who are drawn to this in a way

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:51.110
that I don't think that
if I'd written a book

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:53.490
on Cleveland or Omaha,

00:43:53.490 --> 00:43:54.680
that I would bring out

00:43:54.680 --> 00:43:56.550
Clevelanders and Omahavians.

00:43:57.690 --> 00:44:00.020
But the Pittsburgh diaspora
is exceedingly loyal.

00:44:00.020 --> 00:44:02.800
People who've spent
even a year or two there

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:04.810
think of themselves as
having lived in Pittsburgh,

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:06.650
and it's meaningful to them.

00:44:06.650 --> 00:44:07.710
One thing I would note

00:44:07.710 --> 00:44:10.460
is that if it sometimes
seems to be the case

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:12.170
that everyone knows
someone who used to live

00:44:12.170 --> 00:44:14.370
in Pittsburgh, that's
partly because everyone

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:15.840
does know someone who used
to live in Pittsburgh.

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:18.860
Pittsburgh has a tremendously
populous diaspora

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:20.370
because it only has 1/2 the population

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:21.960
that it had 50 years ago.

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:23.600
So the world is filled with people

00:44:23.600 --> 00:44:25.270
who left Pittsburgh, because for a while,

00:44:25.270 --> 00:44:27.330
its economy cratered and everyone left.

00:44:27.330 --> 00:44:29.070
And now it is thriving again,

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:31.200
but it's a different size city.

00:44:31.200 --> 00:44:33.440
So it's not accidental
that everywhere I go,

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:35.080
there are people who used
to live in Pittsburgh,

00:44:35.080 --> 00:44:36.890
in a way that there
aren't, everywhere I go,

00:44:36.890 --> 00:44:38.650
people who used to live my hometown

00:44:38.650 --> 00:44:40.053
of Springfield, Massachusetts.

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:45.930
From the very beginning,

00:44:45.930 --> 00:44:47.330
there was an Orthodox presence,

00:44:47.330 --> 00:44:48.770
which I think is significant

00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:50.400
in that the infrastructure

00:44:52.425 --> 00:44:53.470
that a neighborhood has often comes

00:44:53.470 --> 00:44:55.260
from the Orthodox, in
terms of building a mikvah,

00:44:55.260 --> 00:44:58.540
a ritual bath, in terms of some shops.

00:44:58.540 --> 00:45:00.050
That might be a kosher butcher,

00:45:00.050 --> 00:45:01.623
that might be a Jewish baker.

00:45:03.170 --> 00:45:05.130
In terms of the fact

00:45:05.130 --> 00:45:07.530
that you will build up a
neighborhood differently

00:45:07.530 --> 00:45:09.480
if it has to be hospitable

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:11.033
to Sabbath-observant Jews.

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:13.830
You can't be too far-flung

00:45:13.830 --> 00:45:15.830
if you're expecting people to walk a mile

00:45:15.830 --> 00:45:17.720
or two or less on Saturday

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:18.850
to get to their synagogue.

00:45:18.850 --> 00:45:21.580
So neighborhoods that cater to

00:45:21.580 --> 00:45:22.980
and welcome observant Jews,

00:45:22.980 --> 00:45:24.510
Sabbath-observant Jews,

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:27.063
are inherently built for density,

00:45:28.098 --> 00:45:31.072
which is why neighborhoods
like West Rogers Park

00:45:31.072 --> 00:45:34.570
in Chicago and Squirrel Hill

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:36.050
and Westville in New Haven

00:45:36.050 --> 00:45:39.440
are often such lovely, properly sized,

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:41.080
human-scale neighborhoods.

00:45:41.080 --> 00:45:42.980
I mean, that's not accidental.

00:45:42.980 --> 00:45:45.080
They also have to have
really interesting zoning

00:45:45.080 --> 00:45:47.270
because you have to be zoned
for residential properties,

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:49.534
but you also have to
allow houses of worship.

00:45:49.534 --> 00:45:50.480
And you probably have to allow

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:51.920
some commercial properties, as well,

00:45:51.920 --> 00:45:53.970
because people are gonna
wanna be able to walk,

00:45:53.970 --> 00:45:55.510
especially if they're
having large families,

00:45:55.510 --> 00:45:58.380
they're gonna wanna walk to their butcher,

00:45:58.380 --> 00:46:00.130
their baker, their candlestick maker.

00:46:00.130 --> 00:46:02.180
So they tend to be neighborhoods

00:46:02.180 --> 00:46:05.540
that resist the suburbanization

00:46:05.540 --> 00:46:08.970
and the kind of strip
mall-ification of America

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:10.200
better than most neighborhoods.

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:12.430
You simply can't do Jewish
life on those models.

00:46:12.430 --> 00:46:16.660
You have to do it on a more
old-fashioned urbanist model.

00:46:16.660 --> 00:46:19.760
And then Squirrel Hill
also has another advantage,

00:46:19.760 --> 00:46:20.920
and I don't fully understand why.

00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:22.830
People have lots of
different theories about why

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:24.620
it ended up working so well,

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:26.950
but it also has many
different sizes of housing.

00:46:26.950 --> 00:46:28.347
I make the point in my book that you

00:46:28.347 --> 00:46:31.750
can get into the Taylor
Allderdice school district

00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:35.760
for under $200,000 or over a million.

00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:36.960
I mean, it has apartments.

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:38.340
It has townhouses.

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:40.000
It has three-family houses.

00:46:40.000 --> 00:46:41.640
It has mansions.

00:46:41.640 --> 00:46:44.630
And so it just developed
really, really beautifully.

00:46:44.630 --> 00:46:46.430
But the most important
thing about Squirrel Hill

00:46:46.430 --> 00:46:47.870
as a neighborhood

00:46:47.870 --> 00:46:50.530
is that there was a key, pivotal moment

00:46:50.530 --> 00:46:53.330
when Jews were heading to the suburbs,

00:46:53.330 --> 00:46:55.640
in the '60s and '70s and '80s,

00:46:55.640 --> 00:46:57.660
as Americans do when they have the money,

00:46:57.660 --> 00:46:59.580
Americans of all ethnicities.

00:46:59.580 --> 00:47:02.570
And the Jewish establishment decided not

00:47:02.570 --> 00:47:04.239
to move the institutions

00:47:04.239 --> 00:47:05.072
of the establishment to the suburbs.

00:47:05.072 --> 00:47:06.140
So unlike, say, New Haven

00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:07.430
and most other American places

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:09.246
where they moved the
Jewish community center

00:47:09.246 --> 00:47:11.780
and the day school to
the first-ring suburb,

00:47:11.780 --> 00:47:13.750
in Pittsburgh, they decided to raise money

00:47:13.750 --> 00:47:16.180
to rebuild and refurbish
all those institutions

00:47:16.180 --> 00:47:18.118
right in Squirrel Hill.

00:47:18.118 --> 00:47:19.319
And that was a very brave decision.

00:47:19.319 --> 00:47:20.655
There's a man named Howard Rieger,

00:47:20.655 --> 00:47:21.488
who was very instrumental in that,

00:47:21.488 --> 00:47:22.700
who now lives in Chicago

00:47:22.700 --> 00:47:24.920
and is a real urbanist activist there.

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:27.650
And there had to be, on
top of all of those aspects

00:47:27.650 --> 00:47:29.977
of good fortune, there also
had to be some intentionality.

00:47:29.977 --> 00:47:32.133
And so a lot came together really well.

00:47:33.470 --> 00:47:34.303
- Great.

00:47:34.303 --> 00:47:35.720
Okay, I was not able to share my screen,

00:47:35.720 --> 00:47:36.700
but-
- That's okay.

00:47:36.700 --> 00:47:39.730
- Can Google the mural in the post office.

00:47:39.730 --> 00:47:42.570
Okay, so a kind of broader question here.

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:44.930
It strikes me, listening to your talk,

00:47:44.930 --> 00:47:45.860
that there are really

00:47:45.860 --> 00:47:49.160
like two competing
narratives going on here.

00:47:49.160 --> 00:47:51.920
There's the one that this is a time

00:47:51.920 --> 00:47:54.360
of the worst antisemitic attack

00:47:54.360 --> 00:47:55.723
in American history.

00:47:56.630 --> 00:47:57.840
That was not the only one.

00:47:57.840 --> 00:47:59.840
As we know, there have been other ones,

00:47:59.840 --> 00:48:02.730
including just a couple weeks ago.

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:06.120
But then, also, you really put

00:48:06.120 --> 00:48:09.187
this emphasis on coming together

00:48:09.187 --> 00:48:11.360
and people showing up for Jews.

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:13.900
So the competing narrative
would be that Jews have,

00:48:13.900 --> 00:48:17.500
in fact, never been more
integrated into American life,

00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:19.783
possibly into any culture's life,

00:48:19.783 --> 00:48:21.540
than they are now.
- Right.

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:24.240
- So how do you think about

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:25.790
those two competing narratives,

00:48:25.790 --> 00:48:28.680
and how does Squirrel Hill, what happened,

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:29.820
help us think about them?

00:48:29.820 --> 00:48:30.653
- Yeah.

00:48:30.653 --> 00:48:33.930
I recently wrote a piece for
"The Wall Street Journal"

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:36.410
in which I pointed out
that social antisemitism

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:38.760
of the kind that kept
Jews out of country clubs

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:40.820
or out of partnerships
at certain law firms,

00:48:40.820 --> 00:48:44.540
as far as I can tell, is
completely gone in America.

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:46.220
I'm sure there are last
vestiges somewhere.

00:48:46.220 --> 00:48:48.367
And I've often said on my podcast,

00:48:48.367 --> 00:48:49.597
"If anyone knows of a country club

00:48:49.597 --> 00:48:51.027
"that truly blackballs Jews

00:48:51.027 --> 00:48:53.727
"or a law firm that truly
won't make a Jewish partner,

00:48:53.727 --> 00:48:55.490
"let me know 'cause I
wanna break that story,"

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:56.757
but I don't think they exist,

00:48:56.757 --> 00:48:59.450
except as urban legend, anymore.

00:48:59.450 --> 00:49:01.400
So social antisemitism of the kind

00:49:01.400 --> 00:49:03.740
that my grandparents would have feared

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:06.429
is basically gone, which is great.

00:49:06.429 --> 00:49:08.520
And that is not necessarily
the case in all,

00:49:08.520 --> 00:49:09.640
say, Western European countries.

00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:11.390
For example, my British and French friends

00:49:11.390 --> 00:49:15.210
tell me there is a lot of
social antisemitism there,

00:49:15.210 --> 00:49:17.180
in the academy and elsewhere.

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:18.330
At the same time,

00:49:18.330 --> 00:49:19.750
things have gotten more violent

00:49:19.750 --> 00:49:21.933
over the past 10 years for Jews.

00:49:22.780 --> 00:49:24.680
Principally, those being attacked are Jews

00:49:24.680 --> 00:49:26.270
who either look visibly Jewish

00:49:26.270 --> 00:49:28.210
because of what they
wear as observant Jews,

00:49:28.210 --> 00:49:30.600
or Jews who enter any Jewish space.

00:49:30.600 --> 00:49:32.730
Whether they are highly
observant themselves or not,

00:49:32.730 --> 00:49:34.260
they've had the courage
to send their children

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:36.680
to Jewish day schools
or to go to synagogue

00:49:36.680 --> 00:49:38.570
or to play squash at the
Jewish community center.

00:49:38.570 --> 00:49:40.800
And the second you enter a Jewish space,

00:49:40.800 --> 00:49:42.770
you are making yourself a target.

00:49:42.770 --> 00:49:45.880
Some people misread my
piece to be counseling Jews

00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:47.310
to just not enter Jewish spaces.

00:49:47.310 --> 00:49:49.500
I mean, it was a very
interesting litmus test

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:50.818
of how people read it,

00:49:50.818 --> 00:49:52.317
because I got a lot of mail saying,

00:49:52.317 --> 00:49:54.017
"Why are you telling us
to just assimilate more

00:49:54.017 --> 00:49:55.770
"and not enter Jewish spaces?"

00:49:55.770 --> 00:49:56.743
And I thought to myself, no, no, no.

00:49:56.743 --> 00:49:59.020
I'm one of those Jews
who goes to Jewish spaces

00:49:59.020 --> 00:50:01.290
all the time, and I'm saying

00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:03.580
we're the ones who are
potentially being victimized.

00:50:03.580 --> 00:50:04.850
That doesn't make us better or worse.

00:50:04.850 --> 00:50:06.550
It's just a sociological fact

00:50:06.550 --> 00:50:09.330
that that's where you'll
find the antisemitism now.

00:50:09.330 --> 00:50:10.550
I also got a lot of pushback from people

00:50:10.550 --> 00:50:12.637
who said, "Well, but then
there's a big exception

00:50:12.637 --> 00:50:15.237
"of college campuses,
where there does seem

00:50:15.237 --> 00:50:17.271
"to be rising antisemitism."

00:50:17.271 --> 00:50:20.730
And far-right and far-left
politics have targeted

00:50:20.730 --> 00:50:23.230
a lot of Jews and made
a lot of Jews afraid

00:50:23.230 --> 00:50:24.870
of being proudly Jewish

00:50:24.870 --> 00:50:28.390
or speaking about Zionism
in the ways they want

00:50:28.390 --> 00:50:29.570
and so forth and so on.

00:50:29.570 --> 00:50:32.550
And I think that's very real.

00:50:32.550 --> 00:50:35.420
So what you saw in Squirrel Hill,

00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:36.890
you see a kind of amity,

00:50:36.890 --> 00:50:39.340
a kind of absence of social antisemitism

00:50:39.340 --> 00:50:41.203
and an absence of social suspicion,

00:50:43.230 --> 00:50:45.640
that meant that the Gentile
allies and neighbors

00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:47.130
really rushed to help Jews.

00:50:47.130 --> 00:50:49.730
And, indeed, the police
rushed to help Jews.

00:50:49.730 --> 00:50:51.150
And that's a very important fact,

00:50:51.150 --> 00:50:53.578
that for most of Jewish history,

00:50:53.578 --> 00:50:54.580
it's like this in "Fiddler on the Roof,"

00:50:54.580 --> 00:50:56.930
God bless and keep the czar
far away from me, right?

00:50:56.930 --> 00:50:59.070
Like, agents of the state generally come

00:50:59.070 --> 00:51:00.990
to either draft you into the czar's army

00:51:00.990 --> 00:51:03.820
or smash your businesses or murder you.

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:05.760
And this was the case in
which agents of the state came

00:51:05.760 --> 00:51:09.570
and put their bodies in
between Jews and bullets.

00:51:09.570 --> 00:51:11.610
And that's a very important
fact about America today,

00:51:11.610 --> 00:51:13.860
that the agents of the
state are on our side.

00:51:15.060 --> 00:51:17.580
But this is a time in which

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:20.380
there have been more violent
attacks on synagogues

00:51:20.380 --> 00:51:23.550
and on, more numerous, still,

00:51:23.550 --> 00:51:24.970
non-lethal but still violent attacks

00:51:24.970 --> 00:51:27.360
on Orthodox Jews on the
streets of Brooklyn,

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:29.390
for example, than in any other five-

00:51:29.390 --> 00:51:30.980
or 10-year period I can think of.

00:51:30.980 --> 00:51:32.510
My general sense is that in a country

00:51:32.510 --> 00:51:35.020
of 330 million people,

00:51:35.020 --> 00:51:36.690
they are statistically meaningless.

00:51:36.690 --> 00:51:38.680
The odds that any given
Jew is going to be punched

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:41.240
or shot are very, very low,
and I don't walk around

00:51:41.240 --> 00:51:43.340
with any anxiety about it myself.

00:51:43.340 --> 00:51:45.277
However, what they do in terms of the mood

00:51:45.277 --> 00:51:46.945
and the fearfulness

00:51:46.945 --> 00:51:48.940
and the way they provide a litmus test

00:51:48.940 --> 00:51:51.850
about how much do Gentiles
react is significant.

00:51:51.850 --> 00:51:53.620
So, for example, I
think there's definitely

00:51:53.620 --> 00:51:56.030
an anti-Jewish bias in a
lot of the mainstream media.

00:51:56.030 --> 00:51:58.230
If you look at the way that Collegeville,

00:51:58.230 --> 00:52:00.450
Colleyville was reported in Texas,

00:52:00.450 --> 00:52:02.760
it got far less attention
than it would have

00:52:02.760 --> 00:52:05.350
if a different, if any
different minority group

00:52:05.350 --> 00:52:08.240
had been held hostage in
their house of worship.

00:52:08.240 --> 00:52:10.820
There's just no question
that Jews don't count as much

00:52:10.820 --> 00:52:13.160
at many media outlets.

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:15.000
So it's very complicated.

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:16.680
- And to follow up on what you were saying

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:19.120
about the Orthodox,

00:52:19.120 --> 00:52:22.450
it strikes me that there
are constant attacks

00:52:22.450 --> 00:52:24.320
on Orthodox Jews now.

00:52:24.320 --> 00:52:26.570
I mean, I just read about more this week.

00:52:26.570 --> 00:52:30.190
And there is not that
same kumbaya (laughs)

00:52:30.190 --> 00:52:34.110
kind of outpouring of,
I mean, or much less

00:52:34.110 --> 00:52:35.905
than it seemed-
- No, there's not.

00:52:35.905 --> 00:52:37.922
And, indeed.
- Yeah.

00:52:37.922 --> 00:52:38.755
(crosstalk)

00:52:38.755 --> 00:52:40.190
- And not even from other Jews.

00:52:40.190 --> 00:52:43.110
I mean, the real shonda,
the real shame there

00:52:43.110 --> 00:52:44.970
is what I see as the secular

00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:47.530
and liberal Jewish willingness to throw

00:52:47.530 --> 00:52:49.400
our more observant brethren and sistren,

00:52:49.400 --> 00:52:51.760
if that a word, under the bus.

00:52:51.760 --> 00:52:54.580
That when it's Haredi, black-hatted Jews

00:52:54.580 --> 00:52:57.360
and their wives being

00:53:01.370 --> 00:53:03.310
punched and kicked

00:53:03.310 --> 00:53:05.770
and attacked verbally and physically

00:53:05.770 --> 00:53:06.773
in Borough Park,

00:53:08.690 --> 00:53:12.060
that less observant and more acculturated

00:53:12.060 --> 00:53:14.950
and assimilated Jews several miles away

00:53:14.950 --> 00:53:16.000
don't care that much.

00:53:19.060 --> 00:53:20.203
Is that antisemitism?

00:53:21.410 --> 00:53:22.770
I mean, yeah, there's some self-loathing,

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:23.603
there's some shame.

00:53:23.603 --> 00:53:25.480
I mean, the anti-Orthodox animus

00:53:25.480 --> 00:53:28.117
among many non-Orthodox
Jews is profound and deep.

00:53:28.117 --> 00:53:29.981
It exists in my own family.

00:53:29.981 --> 00:53:32.030
And we're a family

00:53:32.030 --> 00:53:34.510
of non-Orthodox Jews, for the most part.

00:53:34.510 --> 00:53:37.590
All Jews have some
relatives who are Orthodox.

00:53:37.590 --> 00:53:41.000
And I think that that's
an internal conversation

00:53:41.000 --> 00:53:42.412
that we have to deal with,

00:53:42.412 --> 00:53:43.503
but there's a way in which

00:53:43.503 --> 00:53:46.200
the visibly Jewish Jews

00:53:46.200 --> 00:53:48.140
are lightning rods who draw a lot

00:53:48.140 --> 00:53:51.130
of the violence away, a lot
of the physical violence,

00:53:51.130 --> 00:53:52.670
away from the rest of us,

00:53:52.670 --> 00:53:54.401
and we have to think about that.

00:53:54.401 --> 00:53:55.234
- Yeah.

00:53:56.070 --> 00:53:57.140
So a question

00:53:57.140 --> 00:53:59.660
from someone in the audience
who's a grad student

00:53:59.660 --> 00:54:01.470
who grew up in Squirrel Hill,

00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:05.070
and really appreciates

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:07.760
the relative integration

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:10.520
of different denominations.

00:54:10.520 --> 00:54:14.530
And question is how can
other Jewish communities

00:54:14.530 --> 00:54:17.573
learn or build on Squirrel Hill's example?

00:54:19.620 --> 00:54:20.520
- That's a great question.

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:21.930
I mean, the first thing I always wanna say

00:54:21.930 --> 00:54:23.330
is that I'm always wary

00:54:23.330 --> 00:54:26.170
of making prescriptions
or normative claims

00:54:26.170 --> 00:54:27.713
about how Jews ought to be,

00:54:29.240 --> 00:54:30.410
unless those Jews are my children,

00:54:30.410 --> 00:54:32.320
in which case I'm all about ideas

00:54:32.320 --> 00:54:34.880
for how they should be. (laughs)

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:38.290
I have nothing but ways
to hector my own kids.

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:40.440
But other people's
kids, I try to back off.

00:54:44.090 --> 00:54:47.540
So I'm a journalist
and a lapsed historian,

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:51.600
so I try to just say things how they are.

00:54:51.600 --> 00:54:54.650
It's not my job to tell
people what they ought to do.

00:54:54.650 --> 00:54:56.100
I will say

00:54:56.100 --> 00:54:59.070
that, inevitably, my role,

00:54:59.070 --> 00:55:01.350
my feelings as a Jew and as a citizen

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:03.750
definitely are inescapable,

00:55:03.750 --> 00:55:05.950
especially in a project like this.

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:07.710
And I guess there would
be one basic lesson,

00:55:07.710 --> 00:55:11.010
which is to practice

00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:12.931
what I see Squirrel Hill Jews practicing,

00:55:12.931 --> 00:55:14.280
which is a kind of intrafaith solidarity.

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:16.350
That Squirrel Hill's
remarkable for the extent

00:55:16.350 --> 00:55:19.570
to which secular, Reform,
Reconstructionist,

00:55:19.570 --> 00:55:21.360
Conservative Jews know each other

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:22.400
and know the orthodox.

00:55:22.400 --> 00:55:24.870
And I make the point that you'll find

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:27.300
a Chabad rebbetzin power walking

00:55:27.300 --> 00:55:30.130
with a Reformed Jewish woman
on a Shabbat afternoon.

00:55:30.130 --> 00:55:32.070
And you'll find non-Orthodox Jews

00:55:32.070 --> 00:55:33.060
at the Shabbat tables

00:55:33.060 --> 00:55:35.903
of Orthodox Jews on Friday nights.

00:55:35.903 --> 00:55:38.786
Just the level of knowledge
and the way in which

00:55:38.786 --> 00:55:40.896
they're in each other's
lives in Squirrel Hill,

00:55:40.896 --> 00:55:42.740
partly because of the
intergenerational nature of it.

00:55:42.740 --> 00:55:43.697
Like, I would meet Orthodox who said,

00:55:43.697 --> 00:55:45.787
"Well, my grandpa was Reform,

00:55:45.787 --> 00:55:47.650
"but two generations later,
I've gotten more observant,"

00:55:47.650 --> 00:55:49.160
but they stayed in Squirrel
Hill the whole time.

00:55:49.160 --> 00:55:51.920
So the family ties go back very deep.

00:55:51.920 --> 00:55:53.940
I would just say

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:55.510
you practice hospitality and make sure

00:55:55.510 --> 00:55:57.130
it's a hospitality towards Jews

00:55:57.130 --> 00:55:59.523
who are not like you,
as well as Jews who are.

00:56:00.490 --> 00:56:02.130
It's very simple, but
it's very hard to do.

00:56:02.130 --> 00:56:03.620
And most of us, including me,

00:56:03.620 --> 00:56:04.683
fall down on the job.

00:56:06.590 --> 00:56:07.423
- Yeah.

00:56:07.423 --> 00:56:09.000
So a question about

00:56:11.830 --> 00:56:13.960
what people have done in Squirrel Hill,

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:15.970
but also maybe just more generally,

00:56:15.970 --> 00:56:18.150
to counter the source of the tragedy,

00:56:18.150 --> 00:56:21.140
which is the resurgence
of white nationalism,

00:56:21.140 --> 00:56:22.943
and even outright Nazism.

00:56:24.150 --> 00:56:25.060
Can you say a little bit more?

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:27.800
- I mean, on the very kind of basic level,

00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:28.900
there are security measures,

00:56:28.900 --> 00:56:30.980
that most synagogues now have a guard.

00:56:30.980 --> 00:56:32.340
We didn't used to mostly have a guard.

00:56:32.340 --> 00:56:34.060
My synagogue now has a guard.

00:56:34.060 --> 00:56:36.700
I am a skeptic about how much

00:56:36.700 --> 00:56:38.590
that would matter if a well-armed person

00:56:38.590 --> 00:56:40.243
wanted to come and kill Jews.

00:56:42.830 --> 00:56:44.740
I think it might not matter much.

00:56:44.740 --> 00:56:46.139
The guard might just be the first person

00:56:46.139 --> 00:56:48.589
to get shot, after which
it might be open season.

00:56:49.590 --> 00:56:51.310
I've talked to a lot of
friends who have other ideas

00:56:51.310 --> 00:56:52.960
about what security would work,

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:54.810
rather than a kind of security theater

00:56:54.810 --> 00:56:56.390
that I think we now have, which, often,

00:56:56.390 --> 00:56:57.940
by the way, proves very expensive.

00:56:57.940 --> 00:56:59.400
A lot of money is being wasted

00:56:59.400 --> 00:57:01.293
on bogus security measures right now.

00:57:02.730 --> 00:57:03.563
But

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:08.280
in terms of how to counter the ideology,

00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:10.070
I don't think Jews can counter.

00:57:10.070 --> 00:57:11.550
I don't think anyone.

00:57:11.550 --> 00:57:13.150
I mean, Jews can't fix antisemites.

00:57:13.150 --> 00:57:15.783
Antisemites are soul-sick people,

00:57:16.730 --> 00:57:18.950
and they're not reacting to Jews

00:57:18.950 --> 00:57:20.700
and they're not reacting to Israel.

00:57:22.120 --> 00:57:24.120
And they're not reacting to immigration.

00:57:26.520 --> 00:57:28.430
Because to even go down
that line of thinking,

00:57:28.430 --> 00:57:30.930
I think, suggests that
there's a kind of rationale

00:57:30.930 --> 00:57:32.010
to antisemitism.

00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:33.870
There is, of course, an
internal logic to it.

00:57:33.870 --> 00:57:36.360
In their own brains, it makes some sense.

00:57:36.360 --> 00:57:37.690
But irrational bigotry

00:57:38.930 --> 00:57:42.573
defeats that kind of.

00:57:42.573 --> 00:57:44.307
I mean, it's like cult deprogramming.

00:57:44.307 --> 00:57:45.690
Can it be done?

00:57:45.690 --> 00:57:48.890
Sure, but it's extremely
labor-intensive. (laughs)

00:57:48.890 --> 00:57:51.110
I mean, people should go
read the terrific book,

00:57:51.110 --> 00:57:53.920
Eli Saslow's book "Rising Out of Hatred,"

00:57:53.920 --> 00:57:55.530
about the former Klansman Derek Black,

00:57:55.530 --> 00:57:57.220
who went to New College of Florida,

00:57:57.220 --> 00:57:58.760
where he ended up leaving.

00:57:58.760 --> 00:58:00.610
He'd been raised in a kind of Klan family,

00:58:00.610 --> 00:58:02.193
a very prominent Klan family.

00:58:03.287 --> 00:58:06.660
Again, the book is Saslow, S-A-S-L-O-W,

00:58:06.660 --> 00:58:08.370
and it's called "Rising
Out of Hatred," I think.

00:58:08.370 --> 00:58:10.170
He's a "Washington Post" reporter.

00:58:10.170 --> 00:58:11.134
Best book I've read.

00:58:11.134 --> 00:58:12.130
And you can give it to an 11-year-old.

00:58:12.130 --> 00:58:13.740
Like, it reads very easily.

00:58:13.740 --> 00:58:15.040
And my daughter's read it.

00:58:16.220 --> 00:58:17.230
Totally gripping.

00:58:17.230 --> 00:58:18.280
And this guy went off to college.

00:58:18.280 --> 00:58:19.870
For some reason, he went
to a fairly progressive

00:58:19.870 --> 00:58:21.880
liberal arts college.

00:58:21.880 --> 00:58:25.130
And some Jews started inviting
him to Shabbat dinner.

00:58:25.130 --> 00:58:26.470
And that's actually what cured him

00:58:26.470 --> 00:58:28.960
of his antisemitism,
was just knowing Jews.

00:58:28.960 --> 00:58:30.640
So I think we have to
practice more hospitality

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:32.440
to all humans, but we
should do that, anyway,

00:58:32.440 --> 00:58:35.350
because it's a mitzvah
that we're called on to do.

00:58:35.350 --> 00:58:36.210
I don't think we can worry much about how

00:58:36.210 --> 00:58:38.440
to cure antisemites.

00:58:38.440 --> 00:58:39.273
- Okay, great.

00:58:39.273 --> 00:58:40.400
Well, that brings us

00:58:40.400 --> 00:58:43.040
exactly to the end of our program.

00:58:43.040 --> 00:58:46.060
So I just want to thank you so much.

00:58:46.060 --> 00:58:47.780
On behalf of everyone who you can't see,

00:58:47.780 --> 00:58:49.210
who I'm sure are, they're all

00:58:49.210 --> 00:58:51.410
silently clapping at home,

00:58:51.410 --> 00:58:52.243
(Mark laughs)
thank you.

00:58:52.243 --> 00:58:54.230
I urge everyone to buy the book.

00:58:54.230 --> 00:58:56.750
And, really, thank you again, Mark.

00:58:56.750 --> 00:58:59.130
It was really a fascinating discussion.

00:58:59.130 --> 00:59:01.540
- Well, you and the community around you

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:03.130
have been a huge source of support,

00:59:03.130 --> 00:59:05.030
and I'm excited to meet everyone in person

00:59:05.030 --> 00:59:06.240
when that's possible.

00:59:06.240 --> 00:59:07.360
So thanks, again.

00:59:07.360 --> 00:59:08.193
- Exactly.

00:59:08.193 --> 00:59:09.980
Okay, and thank you to everyone

00:59:09.980 --> 00:59:11.570
who showed up.

00:59:11.570 --> 00:59:12.403
Bye-bye.

